From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] RpiFirmwareDxe: the power-state reply is advisory, not a result

RpiFirmwareSetPowerState() compared the state the firmware echoed back
against the state that was asked for, and turned any disagreement into
EFI_DEVICE_ERROR. On BCM2712 that fires on every boot:

  RpiFirmwareSetPowerState: failed to enable power for device 3
  Couldn't power on USB: Device Error (continuing)

Nothing failed. The legacy device-id power interface is inert on this SoC:
it accepts the request, answers the transaction successfully, and reports
every device in that id space as off. Measured with vcmailbox on a
Raspberry Pi 5 Model B, VideoCore firmware timestamp 1779807685:

  GET_POWER_STATE dev 3 (USB HCD) -> 0x00000000
  GET_POWER_STATE dev 0 (SD card) -> 0x00000000
  SET_POWER_STATE dev 3, state 1  -> 0x00000000
  SET_POWER_STATE dev 3, state 3  -> 0x00000000

Device 0 is the SD controller the board is booting from, so "off" there is
plainly not a description of the hardware. Bit 1 of a reply means "device
does not exist" and is clear throughout, so not even that distinguishes
the cases -- the field simply carries no information on this part. The
WAIT bit makes no difference either, which rules out the one way this
driver's request differs from the kernel's.

Linux does not consult it: rpi_firmware_property() checks the transaction
and the buffer response code, and stops there. Do the same. A genuine
mailbox failure is still caught by the check above this one, which is
unchanged; the disagreement is now a DEBUG_INFO note.

This also silences DwUsbHostDxe, whose best-effort SetPowerState() call
was reporting Device Error for a call that did what it could, and
ConfigDxe's SDHCI call, which has the same reply on this SoC.

Upstream-Status: Submittable [with an RPi5 platform port]
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
diff -urN a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/RpiFirmwareDxe/RpiFirmwareDxe.c b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/RpiFirmwareDxe/RpiFirmwareDxe.c
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/RpiFirmwareDxe/RpiFirmwareDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/RpiFirmwareDxe/RpiFirmwareDxe.c
@@ -444,11 +444,34 @@
     Status = EFI_DEVICE_ERROR;
   }
 
+  //
+  // The state field of the reply is advisory, not a result.
+  //
+  // On BCM2712 it reads back zero for every device in this legacy id space,
+  // including device 0 -- the SD controller, which is demonstrably running.
+  // Deriving an error from it therefore reports a failure that did not
+  // happen. Linux never looks at it: rpi_firmware_property() checks the
+  // transaction and the buffer response code, and nothing else.
+  //
+  // Measured with vcmailbox on a Raspberry Pi 5 Model B, VideoCore firmware
+  // timestamp 1779807685:
+  //
+  //   GET_POWER_STATE dev 3 (USB HCD) -> 0x00000000
+  //   GET_POWER_STATE dev 0 (SD card) -> 0x00000000
+  //   SET_POWER_STATE dev 3, state 1  -> 0x00000000
+  //   SET_POWER_STATE dev 3, state 3  -> 0x00000000
+  //
+  // Bit 1 of a reply means "device does not exist", and it is clear in all
+  // of those: the firmware reports the device as present and off, for
+  // hardware that is present and working. Note the disagreement and carry
+  // on -- a real mailbox failure is still caught above, by the transaction
+  // status and the buffer response code.
+  //
   if (!EFI_ERROR (Status) &&
       PowerState ^ (Cmd->TagBody.PowerState & RPI_MBOX_POWER_STATE_ENABLE)) {
-    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "%a: failed to %sable power for device %d\n",
-      __func__, PowerState ? "en" : "dis", DeviceId));
-    Status = EFI_DEVICE_ERROR;
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_INFO,
+      "%a: device %d reports power state 0x%x after a request to %sable\n",
+      __func__, DeviceId, Cmd->TagBody.PowerState, PowerState ? "en" : "dis"));
   }
   ReleaseSpinLock (&mMailboxLock);
 
